dict(d=D, c="ivx summarizing as intervention", m="say", l=2,
q="Halfway through an interview, you summarize: “So far you’ve told me about the panic attacks, the move to Beirut, and how alone you’ve felt since your brother left.” Besides checking accuracy, what else does a well-timed summary do?",
a="It shows understanding, organizes the material, and can link elements the patient had not connected",
ae="Summaries confirm accuracy, show the patient that they were heard, provide structure and transition, and highlight connections. Selection itself shapes meaning: the interviewer chooses what to place side by side.",
w=[("It mainly allows the interviewer to rest while the patient listens",
"A rest may happen, but this is not the clinical value of summarizing."),
("It is mainly a formality required by documentation standards",
"There is no documentation requirement for spoken summaries."),
("It should be avoided because it interrupts the patient’s flow",
"Poorly timed summaries interrupt, but well-timed ones help organize and deepen.")],
x="In motivational interviewing, “collecting” summaries gather change talk; “linking” summaries connect things the person has said; “transitional” summaries close one topic and move to the next. In psychodynamic work, a summary juxtaposing two themes can function as a gentle, preparatory interpretation.",
s="Miller & Rollnick, Motivational Interviewing; Carlat, The Psychiatric Interview",
h="Summaries: “Collect, Link, Transition.” What you place side by side, you connect."),

